there have been studies of the effects of conscription on long-run earnings,

251
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:41,200
which are generally negative, but you

252
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:43,400
wouldn't expect a conscription was single very much about you,

253
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:45,440
because it applies to everybody.

254
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:50,560
In terms of like the rewards that you get for enrolling the volunteer force,

255
00:11:50,560 --> 00:11:52,440
there must be some papers on it,

256
00:11:52,440 --> 00:11:53,720
but I don't remember seeing them.

257
00:11:53,720 --> 00:11:57,440
But yeah, it does make sense that now you can signal quite a bit by going

258
00:11:57,440 --> 00:12:00,640
and doing the military and getting your honorable discharge,

259
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:02,880
and that looks pretty good. All right.

260
00:12:02,880 --> 00:12:04,920
Although of course, one of the main reasons people do

261
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:07,600
the military now is to get the military to pay for the college.

262
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:11,840
So, that you want to disentangle that,

263
00:12:11,840 --> 00:12:13,080
and then there's a concern, well wait,

264
00:12:13,080 --> 00:12:15,840
you went to the military but you didn't do the free college?

265
00:12:15,840 --> 00:12:17,920
What's wrong with you? Right?

266
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:21,800
And that by itself might ruin all the good signal of the military stuff.

267
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:25,000
So, all right. So, the conscription effect is one.

268
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,960
It is one of the social science literature that is relevant.

269
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:29,680
There's another one on,

270
00:12:29,680 --> 00:12:34,280
we'll just quickly boil that to its credential inflation.

271
00:12:34,280 --> 00:12:38,200
Right. So, there's a lot of work especially in sociology where they

272
00:12:38,200 --> 00:12:40,440
got looked at the typical level of

273
00:12:40,440 --> 00:12:43,120
education that people in different jobs have had over time.

274
00:12:43,120 --> 00:12:45,440
And as you might guess,

275
00:12:45,440 --> 00:12:49,440
there's been some expansion of the modern economy into more high-tech jobs,

276
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:51,920
where people generally might think that people possibly

277
00:12:51,920 --> 00:12:56,200
need more education or do those jobs like computer science, something like that.

278
00:12:56,200 --> 00:12:59,520
But there's a lot of jobs that seem totally technologically

279
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:02,400
stagnant or maybe even easier than they used to be.

280
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:05,880
Like being a waiter today is probably easier than it was in 1950.

281
00:13:05,880 --> 00:13:08,200
In 1950, you probably would do,

282
00:13:08,200 --> 00:13:10,920
you would add up the customer's bill at their table.

283
00:13:10,920 --> 00:13:13,480
Right. Things like that. All right.

284
00:13:13,480 --> 00:13:17,920
And yet, we can see is that there's been a large rise in education of workers,

285
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:21,240
even in industries that it seems like they are no more

286
00:13:21,240 --> 00:13:24,840
cognitively demanding than they used to be or maybe they are even less so.

287
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:26,600
Right. And in general,

288
00:13:26,600 --> 00:13:28,240
what these studies find is that,

289
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:32,200
so when they go and compare the education of people in different jobs over time,

290
00:13:32,200 --> 00:13:37,000
is that only about 20 percent of the rise in education of

291
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:40,200
the workforce can be accounted for by more cognitively demanding jobs.

292
00:13:40,200 --> 00:13:45,640
Eighty percent comes from the education required to get a job rising over time,

293
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:48,040
get a given job rising over time.

294
00:13:48,040 --> 00:13:52,000
So, now it is very common that a waiter in a nice restaurant,

295
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:53,800
or a concierge in a fancy hotel,

296
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:55,920
or a secretary with a college degree.

297
00:13:55,920 --> 00:13:58,680
Fifty years ago, this would have been crazy.

298
00:13:58,680 --> 00:14:03,400
Right. So, again, this is hard to understand in terms of human capital.

299
00:14:03,400 --> 00:14:07,120
Because look, if a high school degree is enough to train someone to be a waiter in 1950,

300
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:10,400
it should still be enough. Why not?

301
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:12,600
And yet, if what's going on is that you were

302
00:14:12,600 --> 00:14:16,040
signaling that you were in the top third of workers by your education,

303
00:14:16,040 --> 00:14:19,040
then if the educational attainment of the population rises,

304
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:21,560
you need more to show that you're in the top third.

305
00:14:21,560 --> 00:14:25,480
So, the signaling model has this built-in rat race idea that,

306
00:14:25,480 --> 00:14:27,120
the more everybody else has,

307
00:14:27,120 --> 00:14:30,040
the more you have to have or else you look bad.

308
00:14:30,040 --> 00:14:34,560
So, this is, again,

309
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:37,600
like to me, is in a way some of the most convincing evidence.

310
00:14:37,600 --> 00:14:39,000
Again, like economists will say,

311
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:40,840
non-invented here, sociologists did it.

312
00:14:40,840 --> 00:14:43,200
But it's a pretty simple method where you just go and get

313
00:14:43,200 --> 00:14:47,400
Bureau of Labor Statistics ratings for what skills you need to do a job at different times.

314
00:14:47,400 --> 00:14:51,560
They do try to consider the possibility that the job itself has changed.

315
00:14:51,560 --> 00:14:54,760
But again, there's just a lot of very stagnant sectors of the economy,

316
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:58,800
where what workers do is very similar to what they did in the past.

317
00:14:58,960 --> 00:15:05,200
So, there's that. Then, there is a whole literature on what is called,

318
00:15:05,200 --> 00:15:09,560
let's see, like national returns versus personal returns.

319
00:15:09,560 --> 00:15:14,200
All right. So, there's a lot of research, of course,

320
00:15:14,200 --> 00:15:16,360
on when an individual gets one more year of education,

321
00:15:16,360 --> 00:15:19,520
how much does this raise the individual's earnings? So, there's that.

322
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:22,560
But over in macro, there is a parallel literature,

323
00:15:22,560 --> 00:15:26,360
where they say if the workforce in the country gets one more year of education,

324
00:15:26,360 --> 00:15:29,320
how much does that raise the earnings of the country?

325
00:15:29,320 --> 00:15:33,280
So, just like you can go and regress personal income on personal education,

326
00:15:33,280 --> 00:15:36,160
you can regress national income on national education.

327
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:38,680
This has been done. Right now,

328
00:15:38,680 --> 00:15:41,920
the human capital model basically predicts that you'll get the same results.

329
00:15:41,920 --> 00:15:44,960
So, if an individual goes to school and gets 10 percent more

330
00:15:44,960 --> 00:15:47,080
productivity and that raises the wage by 10 percent,

331
00:15:47,080 --> 00:15:49,760
if the whole country gets one more year of education,

332
00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:51,520
that raises the productivity of the country by 10 percent,

333
00:15:51,520 --> 00:15:54,320
and the country's income will go up by 10 percent.

334
00:15:54,320 --> 00:15:55,720
Signaling, on the other hand,

335
00:15:55,720 --> 00:15:57,360
says it's not going to work that way,

336
00:15:57,360 --> 00:16:00,320
and that there's going to be a lower return for

337
00:16:00,320 --> 00:16:04,440
countries to raising their education than for individuals. All right.

338
00:16:04,440 --> 00:16:07,960
Now, there is a pretty big literature on national returns,

339
00:16:07,960 --> 00:16:11,000
and their estimates are all over the place.

340
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,880
They had, there's many different data sets that have been used,

341
00:16:13,880 --> 00:16:16,520
but every single data set used finds

342
00:16:16,520 --> 00:16:19,360
the national return is way below the personal return.

343
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:22,440
Every single one. All right.

344
00:16:22,440 --> 00:16:26,480
Now, the reaction of people who are doing the research is normally to say,

345
00:16:26,480 --> 00:16:28,400
we're doing something wrong.

346
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:32,880
What's wrong? Why are we getting the wrong answer?

347
00:16:32,880 --> 00:16:35,240
Right. And in my book,

348
00:16:35,240 --> 00:16:39,200
I do actually find some smoking gun papers where they literally actually

349
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:44,160
say why this data set is better because it gets a higher number for the return,

350
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:48,840
which is what's called, what kind of reasoning is that again?

351
00:16:48,840 --> 00:16:51,400
Circular reasoning.

352
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:53,120
Circular reasoning. Yes.

353
00:16:53,120 --> 00:16:56,120
Especially when there is a simple model that predicts

354
00:16:56,120 --> 00:16:58,600
that the reward for educating of our country is

355
00:16:58,600 --> 00:17:01,360
raising their education levels will be lower than for individuals.

356
00:17:01,360 --> 00:17:03,880
And when one individual raises education,

357
00:17:03,880 --> 00:17:06,640
he stands out from the pack and looks better and gets makes more money.

358
00:17:06,640 --> 00:17:08,560
But if everyone in the country gets extra years,

359
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:10,840
everyone in the country can't stand out.

360
00:17:10,840 --> 00:17:13,520
And so it just raises the amount.

361
00:17:13,520 --> 00:17:15,640
So anyway, so I did find one paper,

362
00:17:15,640 --> 00:17:18,720
they actually, they were pushing their own new data set,

363
00:17:18,720 --> 00:17:20,840
but it was a great paper because they went and did

364
00:17:20,840 --> 00:17:24,160
the same thing for all eight existing data sets.

365
00:17:24,160 --> 00:17:26,480
And all eight of them,

366
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:28,240
they came up with the very highest number,

367
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:32,840
which was that the national return is about half of a normal estimate of the personal return.

368
00:17:32,840 --> 00:17:38,600
And then, but the average payoff that they got out of all the data sets was that,

369
00:17:38,600 --> 00:17:42,960
you know, you raise the education of your whole nation's workforce by a year,

370
00:17:42,960 --> 00:17:45,640
raises earnings by 1.3 percent,

371
00:17:45,640 --> 00:17:49,400
when for an individual it would typically be like 10 percent.

372
00:17:49,400 --> 00:17:52,840
The only decent answer that I've heard to this objection is,

373
00:17:52,840 --> 00:17:54,760
well, you have to understand that there's a lot of these countries,

374
00:17:54,760 --> 00:17:56,280
the education system is terrible.

375
00:17:56,280 --> 00:17:59,000
So, like, Lampritch is this great book called,

376
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,600
A Schooling Ain't Learning, where he talks about

377
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:05,000
all the countries would have high absenteeism of teachers, like India.

378
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:06,680
So you say, well, it would make sense,

379
00:18:06,680 --> 00:18:07,800
if teachers don't even show up,

380
00:18:07,800 --> 00:18:09,280
it's not going to be any good, right?

381
00:18:09,280 --> 00:18:13,840
But that predicts that schooling in India would raise

382
00:18:13,840 --> 00:18:16,040
neither personal nor national income.

383
00:18:16,040 --> 00:18:19,000
And there's plenty of evidence from India saying that schooling in India

384
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,800
raises income despite the fact that teachers are barely showing up sometimes.

385
00:18:22,800 --> 00:18:26,120
All right. So, again, it fits very nicely with signaling.

386
00:18:26,120 --> 00:18:28,080
And again, like, well, why would it signal to go to school,

387
00:18:28,080 --> 00:18:31,600
to finish school when the teachers don't show up half the time?

388
00:18:31,600 --> 00:18:34,800
It's like, well, think about how demoralizing it is to show up to school,

389
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:36,480
when the teacher's not there most of the time.

390
00:18:36,480 --> 00:18:39,280
We take a very smart,

391
00:18:39,280 --> 00:18:43,120
smart, hardworking, informed person to keep going when the teacher doesn't go.

392
00:18:43,120 --> 00:18:45,440
So, maybe that's the story. All right.

393
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:47,760
So, that and then finally,

394
00:18:47,760 --> 00:18:52,080
there is a literature usually called the employer learning literature.

395
00:18:52,080 --> 00:18:54,280
Where they go and try to basically,

396
00:18:54,280 --> 00:18:56,400
or it's not learning, it's learning.

397
00:18:56,400 --> 00:19:02,800
Learning literature. This is where the researchers go and get some measure of your true ability,

398
00:19:02,800 --> 00:19:06,040
like IQ, they assume it's not accessible to employers.

399
00:19:06,040 --> 00:19:09,240
And then they see, well,

400
00:19:09,240 --> 00:19:13,720
how does the return to ability, to your true ability,

401
00:19:13,720 --> 00:19:15,920
compare to the return to education over time?

402
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:19,080
And normally, what they find is that the longer you work,

403
00:19:19,080 --> 00:19:22,760
the lower the return to education and the higher the return to ability.

404
00:19:22,760 --> 00:19:24,600
And they normally interpret this as learning.

405
00:19:24,600 --> 00:19:26,000
So, basically, this means, suppose that you're

406
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:27,920
a really smart person who drops out of high school.

407
00:19:27,920 --> 00:19:30,320
When you first, the first job you get,

408
00:19:30,320 --> 00:19:34,640
you'll basically earn a very typical high school dropout's wage.

409
00:19:34,640 --> 00:19:37,400
But over time, 10 years later,

410
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:40,160
you will be doing better than other high school dropouts on average.

411
00:19:40,160 --> 00:19:45,480
The market will gradually detect your superior ability and then go and reward it for you.

412
00:19:45,480 --> 00:19:48,920
And the penalty for your poor education gradually goes away.

413
00:19:48,920 --> 00:19:51,160
So, at least gradually diminishes anyway.

414
00:19:51,160 --> 00:19:53,880
All right. So, there's this literature here.

415
00:19:53,880 --> 00:19:56,120
And what did it find? Oh, yeah, I'm going to.

416
00:19:56,120 --> 00:20:08,680
General Results, it finds that if you are someone who is...


